(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-chemistry-panels-quiz"]={"title":"Chemistry Panels, Renal Function & Electrolytes Quiz 1 — PDM I Exam 1","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is an electrocardiogram part of the ketoacidosis workup?","choices":["To confirm the diagnosis of ketoacidosis","To look for the cardiac effects of a raised potassium","To exclude a myocardial infarction as the precipitant","To measure the degree of dehydration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the ketoacidosis workup includes an electrocardiogram for potassium effects, because both high and low potassium can cause life-threatening arrhythmias. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which four core tests evaluate water and electrolyte balance?","choices":["Albumin, total protein, prothrombin time, and bilirubin","Serum sodium, serum osmolality, urine sodium and osmolality, and the urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio","Serum sodium, serum potassium, serum calcium, serum magnesium, and serum phosphate","Serum glucose, bicarbonate, the anion gap, and ketones"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — serum sodium and osmolality, urine sodium and osmolality, and the urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio together locate the problem. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Under the acute illness indication, which two situations call for a chemistry panel?","choices":["Fever and a suspected bloodstream infection","Chest trauma and a suspected rib fracture","Headache and a suspected migraine","Dehydration and a suspected acid-base disorder"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — dehydration and a suspected acid-base disorder are the acute illness indications; a urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio above 20 suggests hypovolemia. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Where does calcium sit among the panel components?","choices":["On its own, as the mineral","With sodium and potassium as an electrolyte","With the liver and protein markers","With blood urea nitrogen as a kidney marker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the panel groups glucose as fuel, the four electrolytes for acid-base, urea nitrogen and creatinine for the kidney, and calcium alone as the mineral. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why is bicarbonate reported on a chemistry panel as carbon dioxide?","choices":["The laboratory measures dissolved carbon dioxide gas directly","Bicarbonate is unstable and must be converted before measurement","It reflects the carbon dioxide cleared by the lungs that minute","The panel reports total carbon dioxide, which is mostly serum bicarbonate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the panel reports total carbon dioxide, which is mostly serum bicarbonate, the primary extracellular buffer. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which hormone lowers blood glucose, and which raise it?","choices":["Insulin lowers it; antidiuretic hormone raises it","Insulin lowers it; glucagon and the counter-regulatory hormones raise it","Glucagon lowers it; insulin and cortisol raise it","Cortisol lowers it; insulin and adrenaline together raise it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — insulin lowers glucose, glucagon and the counter-regulatory hormones raise it, and the kidney also contributes through gluconeogenesis. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Using the standard formula, what is the normal range for the anion gap?","choices":["8 to 12 milliequivalents per liter","10 to 14 milliequivalents per liter","4 to 8 milliequivalents per liter","12 to 20 milliequivalents per liter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — 8 to 12 milliequivalents per liter is the normal range when potassium is left out of the calculation. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What effect does marked hyperglycemia have on the measured sodium?","choices":["It leaves it unchanged but raises the potassium","It makes the sodium unmeasurable until the glucose is corrected","It lowers it, by dilution","It raises it, by drawing water into the cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — marked hyperglycemia lowers the measured sodium by dilution, by about 1.6 to 2 milliequivalents per liter for each 100 milligrams per deciliter of glucose above normal. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 13 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"What is the other common name for the comprehensive metabolic panel?","choices":["Chem-10","Chem-14","Chem-8","Chem-20"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the panels are commonly named for the number of tests they carry, and the comprehensive panel holds fourteen. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Besides reduced kidney clearance, which of these raises blood urea nitrogen?","choices":["Reduced muscle mass","Gastrointestinal bleeding","Low dietary protein intake","Overhydration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — urea nitrogen is non-specific: besides reduced renal clearance, dehydration, gastrointestinal bleeding, high protein intake and catabolic states raise it. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does a chemistry panel measure?","choices":["Red cells, white cells, and platelets with their indices","Clotting factor activity together with the time taken for a clot to form in the sample","Hormone levels from the thyroid, adrenal, and pituitary glands","Metabolites, electrolytes, kidney markers, and in the expanded version liver and protein markers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the panel measures metabolites, electrolytes, kidney markers and, in the expanded version, liver and protein markers: a snapshot of chemical balance and organ function. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which three factors influence creatinine and can make it misleading?","choices":["Protein intake, hydration, and recent bleeding","Liver function, bilirubin, and albumin","Time of day, posture, and fasting state","Muscle mass, age, and sex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — creatinine is influenced by muscle mass, age and sex, so it may underestimate kidney dysfunction in patients with low muscle mass. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What happens to serum potassium in alkalosis?","choices":["Potassium is unaffected by alkalosis","Potassium shifts into cells, so the serum level falls","Potassium shifts out of cells, so the serum level rises","Potassium is retained by the kidney, so the serum level rises"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — alkalosis shifts potassium into cells and lowers the serum level, while acidosis shifts it out of cells and raises the serum level. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How should blood urea nitrogen be read?","choices":["Interpret it only when the patient is fasting","Interpret it only if the estimated filtration rate is normal","Interpret it alongside creatinine, not alone","Interpret it alone, since creatinine is less specific"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — urea nitrogen alone cannot distinguish dehydration or a protein load from kidney failure; the ratio to creatinine is what separates them. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"What is blood urea nitrogen, and where is it produced and cleared?","choices":["A nitrogenous waste product of protein metabolism, produced by the liver and cleared by the kidneys","A nitrogenous waste product of muscle metabolism, produced and cleared by the kidneys","A breakdown product of red blood cells, conjugated by the liver","An enzyme released from injured hepatocytes into the blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — urea nitrogen is a nitrogenous waste of protein metabolism, produced by the liver and cleared by the kidneys, so it rises with reduced renal clearance. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Serum sodium represents a balance between which two processes?","choices":["Bone storage and bone release","Hepatic synthesis and renal clearance","Oral intake and renal excretion","Gastrointestinal absorption and biliary loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — serum sodium is a balance between oral intake and renal excretion, and sodium salts are the major determinant of extracellular osmolality. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which is a synthetic product of the liver, and therefore a measure of what the liver is making?","choices":["Albumin","Alkaline phosphatase","Total bilirubin","Aspartate aminotransferase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — albumin is synthesized only by the liver, and with a half-life of about three weeks a low value marks chronic liver disease. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Where is sodium the major cation?","choices":["In the cerebrospinal fluid specifically","In the biliary and pancreatic secretions","In the extracellular fluid","In the intracellular fluid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — sodium is the major extracellular cation. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What comes before an extensive workup on a borderline abnormal result?","choices":["Repeat or confirm the abnormality","Proceed straight to imaging","Treat empirically and recheck afterwards","Refer to the relevant specialist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — normal ranges are the mean plus or minus two standard deviations, so some healthy people fall outside them by chance; confirm before an extensive workup. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"On a chemistry panel, what does the abbreviation Cr stand for?","choices":["Chromium","Creatine kinase","C-reactive protein","Creatinine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — on the panel Cr is creatinine, the waste product of muscle creatine metabolism that the kidneys filter, paired with urea nitrogen. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which two mechanisms control water handling, and therefore serum sodium?","choices":["Thirst and antidiuretic hormone","Renin and angiotensin","Aldosterone and atrial natriuretic peptide","Insulin and the catecholamines"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — serum sodium reflects water balance, which thirst and antidiuretic hormone control, while total-body sodium governs extracellular volume. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 9 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"A low bicarbonate is the trigger to do what next?","choices":["Calculate the anion gap","Order an arterial blood gas before anything else","Repeat the panel to exclude a laboratory error","Give intravenous bicarbonate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the gap separates acidosis caused by added acid from acidosis caused by bicarbonate loss, which is the first branch point in finding the cause. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What causes a cholestatic pattern?","choices":["Viral hepatitis, alcohol, ischemia, and drug injury","Gilbert syndrome and hemolysis","Heart failure and sepsis","Bile duct obstruction, gallstones, and primary biliary cholangitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — bile duct obstruction, gallstones and primary biliary cholangitis raise alkaline phosphatase out of proportion to the transaminases. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 17 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Why do small fluctuations in potassium matter?","choices":["Small changes reliably indicate that the sample was taken or handled incorrectly","Small changes are usually meaningless and can be ignored","Small changes only matter in patients with kidney disease","Small changes in the serum level carry significant physiological consequences"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — potassium maintains membrane electrical potential, especially in neuromuscular tissue, so small changes can cause life-threatening arrhythmias. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What usually follows any single or combined abnormality on a panel?","choices":["Referral to the relevant specialist","Repeating the whole panel every day","Confirmatory testing","Immediate treatment of the abnormal value"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a single abnormal value is a screening signal rather than a diagnosis, so it is confirmed before it is acted on. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"When carbon dioxide and hydrogen ions rise, bicarbonate moves out of the cell. What does chloride do?","choices":["It binds to hemoglobin and is carried to the lungs for excretion","It shifts back into the cell to preserve electrical neutrality","It shifts out of the cell alongside bicarbonate","It is excreted by the kidney within minutes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — when bicarbonate moves out of the cell, chloride shifts back into the cell to maintain electrical neutrality, so the two move reciprocally. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which syndrome shows liver and kidney failure at once?","choices":["Diabetic ketoacidosis","Nephrotic syndrome","Hepatorenal syndrome","Cardiorenal syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — hepatorenal syndrome shows both liver and kidney failure, much as ketoacidosis produces electrolyte and renal derangements at the same time. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How is the magnitude of a transaminase rise banded?","choices":["Mild under five times, moderate five to fifteen times, and severe over fifteen times the upper limit","Mild under two times, moderate two to ten times, and severe over ten times the upper limit","Mild under ten times, moderate ten to fifty times, and severe over fifty times the upper limit","Mild under three times, moderate three to twenty times, and severe over twenty times the upper limit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a rise under five times the upper limit is mild, five to fifteen times is moderate, and over fifteen times is severe. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 17 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"What is the standard formula for the anion gap?","choices":["Chloride plus bicarbonate minus sodium","Sodium minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate","Sodium minus chloride, without subtracting bicarbonate","The sum of sodium and potassium minus chloride"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the gap is sodium minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate, and what remains represents the unmeasured anions. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Where does creatinine come from?","choices":["It is filtered from dietary creatine without being metabolized","It is produced by the kidney tubules during filtration","It is a waste product of muscle creatine metabolism","It is a waste product of protein metabolism produced by the liver"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — creatinine is a waste product of muscle creatine metabolism, filtered by the kidneys, and a more specific renal marker than urea nitrogen. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"}]};
